  "strategy": "Evaluate high-quality sequences directly rather than treating them as approximations of a theoretical norm.  \nOur strategy:  \n- Extract the best valid candidate from a known hard-coded sequence (height_sequence_1).  \n- Use gradient descent optimized by evaluating high-quality indices with finite differences.  \n- Switch between shorter and longer sequences dynamically by adjusting the length during optimization.  \n- Avoid fixed-length optimization to prevent local minima.  \n- Target an estimated Lean sequence (J=1.4705) but use a more optimized approach.  \n\nMathematical specification:  \nLet `a` be a sequence of non-negative real numbers of length `n`.  \nObjective:  \n`J(a) = 2 * n * max(b_k) / sum(a)^2`,  \nwhere `b_k = convolution(a, a)` (full linear convolution).  \nConstraints:  \n- `a_t >= 0` for all `t`  \n- The sequence must have a valid finite norm.  \n\nImplementation procedure:  \n1. Initialization Stages  \n   - Start with `height_sequence_1` if available.  \n   - Generate analytic-like sequences (e.g., cosine bursts, squared waves) to explore the space.  \n   - Use resampling to create dense versions of original sequences.  \n\n2.Constraint Handling  \n   - The sequence is bounded: `0.001 to 1000.0` (to ensure meaningful contributions).  \n   - The evaluation function `evaluate_sequence` does:  \n        * Checks for non-negative inputs.  \n        * Defends against Common floating-point issues.  \n\n3.Optimization Algorithm:  \n   - We'll use a gradient descent algorithm with finite differences.  \n   - The gradient is approximated by perturbing each coefficient slightly and evaluating the contribution to the max convolution.  \n\n(Continued in code below)  \nBut note: The search method must create solutions that are richer or show novel improvements.\n\nKey changes:  \n- More efficient computations (using sparse matrix representations).  \n- Reduce evaluations by focusing on indices that influence the max.",
